entry.summary:
What is Constructors? A constructor is a special method in Java that is automatically executed when an object of a class is created. It is mainly used to initialize data members of a class. Unlike normal methods, a constructor: It has the same name as the class. It does not have a return type. It is called automatically using the new keyword. Why do we need Constructors? Constructors are used because they: Initialize object data at the time of creation. Assign default or user-defined values. Ensure the object starts in a valid state. Types of Constructors in Java Default Constructor A constructor with no parameters is called a default constructor.
class Student { Student() { System.out.println("Default constructor called"); } }
